  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the temperature of the substance is $34$ degrees Fahrenheit, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34 & =250-{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =216 \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing when $t=6$ minutes is equal to ${F}^{\u2032}(6).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $F(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250-{t}^{3})=-3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=6$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{\u2032}(6)=-3(6)^{2}=-108.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the temperature of the substance is changing when $t=6$ minutes is $-108$ degrees Fahrenheit per minute.",
